      'To evaluate Muhammad ﷺ fairly, classical jurist Al-Mawardi applied a simple logical grid: a claimant to prophethood is either lying, deluded, or truthful. Consider the historical record preserved by friend and foe alike.\n\nFor forty years before his call, Mecca’s merchants called him Al-Amin—the Trustworthy. When Quraysh leaders offered him kingship, fortune, and prestige simply to cease preaching Tawhid, he refused. Later, on the very day his beloved infant son Ibrahim passed away, a solar eclipse darkened Medina. Grieving onlookers cried that the sun eclipsed in mourning for the Prophet’s child. A fraud would have harvested a lifetime of awe from that coincidence. Instead, he stepped out and declared: "The sun and the moon are two signs of Allah; they do not eclipse for the death or life of anyone."\n\nNeither an impostor chasing power nor a fragmented mind lost in delusion behaves like this—much less sustains twenty-three years of moral clarity, mercy, and revelation that reshaped human civilization. Eliminate the impossible, and truth stands in plain light.',
